  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both in place, and the rule is enabled. Its first run succeeded with no errors and no alerts.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The \"Kibana Sample Data Logs\" data view uses `timestamp` as its time field. It covers:\n- **Request:** `url`, `request`, `extension`, `referer`, and `response`, the HTTP status code stored as text, with `response.keyword` for exact matching.\n- **Size:** `bytes`.\n- **Client:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, and `machine.ram`.\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, and `geo.coordinates`.\n- **Other:** `host` and `tags`, plus an `hour_of_day` field calculated from the timestamp.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-6cb646ac Web logs overview\"** (id `99c4f0da-7891-4d93-9dc3-e1aad350f74d`, default time range last 7 days). It has 8 panels:\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), average response size, and total bytes served.\n- Requests over time, as stacked bars split by response code.\n- A donut chart of requests by response code.\n- A table of the top 10 requested URLs.\n- A donut chart of requests by operating system.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-6cb646ac 5xx spike\"** (id `5f6dcb09-52df-48b6-a2ae-7bf58764effe`). It's an Elasticsearch query rule on the web-logs data view:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It fires when more than 10 matching documents arrive in the last 5 minutes.\n- It counts requests matching `response.keyword >= 500 and response.keyword < 600`, which picks up every 5xx code.\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone. If you want an email or Slack message, you'll need to add a connector to it.\n\nThe \"no alerts\" result on the first run only means there weren't more than 10 5xx requests in that 5-minute window. I didn't separately run the query to check that it matches 5xx documents.",
